      "content": "<h3>add, addLast and offer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>boolean add(E e);  void addLast(E e);  boolean offer(E e)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> <code>add</code> and <code>offer</code> return <code>true</code>; <code>addLast</code> returns nothing</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> All three put the element at the <strong>end</strong> of the list. Use <code>add</code> when you think \"list\", <code>offer</code> when you think \"queue\", and <code>addLast</code> when you think \"deque\".</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> They all call the same internal <code>linkLast</code>: create a node, point the old last node's <code>next</code> at it, and update <code>last</code>. A LinkedList has no capacity limit, so <code>offer</code> never returns <code>false</code> here (it can for bounded queues).</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// LinkedList add / addLast / offer: three names for \"append\"\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\n\npublic class LinkedListAddDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        LinkedList<String> forts = new LinkedList<>();\n\n        forts.add(\"Raigad\");           // list style\n        forts.addLast(\"Sinhagad\");     // deque style\n        boolean accepted = forts.offer(\"Torna\");   // queue style, returns true when accepted\n\n        System.out.println(\"offer returned : \" + accepted);\n        System.out.println(\"List           : \" + forts);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// offer returned : true\n// List           : [Raigad, Sinhagad, Torna]"
      ]
