      "content": "<h3>add(int index, E e)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>void add(int index, E element)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> nothing (void)</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n) to reach the index</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Inserts the element at a position. Linking the new node is instant, but Java must first <strong>walk</strong> to that position, one node at a time.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> The walk starts from whichever end is closer (<code>index &lt; size/2</code> goes from the front, otherwise from the back), so the average cost is about n/4 steps. For an insertion that is far from both ends an ArrayList is usually still faster because its shifting is a native block copy.</p>",
