      "content": "<h3>peek, peekFirst and peekLast</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>E peek();  E peekFirst();  E peekLast()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> the element, or <code>null</code> if the list is empty</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Look at an end <strong>without removing it</strong>; if the list is empty you get <code>null</code>, not an exception. <code>peek()</code> and <code>peekFirst()</code> look at the front, <code>peekLast()</code> at the back.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> These are the \"safe\" counterparts of <code>getFirst</code>/<code>getLast</code>. Because LinkedList allows <code>null</code> elements, a <code>null</code> result is ambiguous: it could be \"empty\" or \"the element is null\". Check <code>isEmpty()</code> if you store nulls.</p>",
